# .allow-workspace-private — RETIRED (2026-04-27)

The `.allow-workspace-private` marker file was removed from this repo.

## Why removed

The marker tells `~/cyborg/scripts/check-no-leak.sh` to skip path-pattern +
IP-pattern checks because workspace-private dot-dirs are legitimate tracked
content in private workspace repos.

**This repo is NOT a private workspace.** Even though `github.com/thewhyman/
career-os-plugin` is a private GitHub repo, the contents ship PUBLICLY:

- Career-OS users install the plugin via `claude plugin add career-os`
- Plugin contents (skills, hooks, migrations) land on every user's machine
- Anything in this repo is effectively public-facing surface area

The leak gate exists to catch private content (workspace-private dot-dirs,
internal IP terms, PII) that should NEVER ship to a public-facing surface.
This repo IS that surface for thousands of plugin users — the leak gate
must run with full strictness, not workspace-private leniency.

## What still applies

The leak gate's other invariants (credential / PII pattern checks, secrets
detection) ALWAYS fire regardless of any marker. Those were never gated by
this file; they fire on every repo.

## What changed in practice

After this commit, every leak-gate run on this repo enforces:

1. No workspace-private dot-dirs (`.career-os/`, `.gemini/`, `.codex/`,
   workspace-local `.claude/` folders, etc.) — these are USER data, not
   plugin data.
2. No internal IP terms (`thewhyman`, `xHumanOS`, `xTeamOS` brand-only
   references that aren't public marketing surface) outside of intentional
   marketing files.
3. No PII patterns (real names of real people without explicit consent).

If the leak gate flags a legitimate plugin file with these checks, the fix
is to sanitize the file or refactor — NOT to add a marker that disables
the check.

## Origin

2026-04-27 — caught during cyborg session that audited the marker's purpose
against the repo's actual distribution model. The marker was added during
the `.career-os/` leak hardening pass on the assumption this was a private
workspace; the audit revealed plugin distribution is public-facing.

This file (`.RETIRED` suffix) is kept as documentation so future agents
understand why the marker is gone and don't restore it on instinct.
